{
  "schemaVersion": 3,
  "id": "shelter",
  "name": "Shelter",
  "tagline": "Deep Work under a veil of tuned breath, for noisy places.",
  "description": "Deep Work's pulse and pad under a veil: breath through tuned pipes on the chord's notes, over soft air across the band speech is understood in. It fills that band steadily, so nearby voices blur into it.",
  "use": {
    "name": "Shield",
    "for": "Working where people are talking: a café, an open office, a busy house.",
    "why": "Nearby speech is the most disruptive sound for mental work, and the more of it you can follow, the worse. Intelligible speech and lyrics cut reading comprehension and writing far more than any instrumental sound, and office studies find performance falls as speech becomes intelligible. A steady sound covering the frequencies intelligibility depends on (most of it between 1 and 4 kHz) lets less of it through, and steady sound itself costs little. Here tuned breath on the chord's notes, over soft air across that band, fills it steadily under Deep Work's beat: its quietest moments there are far fuller than Deep Work's, whose energy in that band comes only in bursts. The masking evidence is strong; but noise slightly hurts people without attention difficulties when it is quiet, so this is for when there are voices around. Otherwise, play Deep Work."
  },
